```js
function validateAge(input) {
  const val = Number(input.value);
  return !isNaN(val) && val >= 18 && val <= 60;
}
document.getElementById('age').addEventListener('blur', function(e) {
  e.target.style.borderColor = validateAge(e.target) ? 'green' : 'red';
});
```
A wrong answer (e.g., using `onchange` instead of `blur`, or checking `input.value` without `Number()`) reveals confusion between event types (`change` fires on commit, `blur` on focus loss) and type coercion pitfalls (`"25" >= 18` works but `"abc" >= 18` is false, yet `Number("abc")` is NaN).

```html
<textarea id="msg" maxlength="200" rows="4" cols="50"></textarea>
<div id="counter">200 characters remaining</div>
<script>
  const ta = document.getElementById('msg');
  const ct = document.getElementById('counter');
  ta.addEventListener('input', function() {
    const rem = 200 - ta.value.length;
    ct.textContent = rem + ' characters remaining';
    ct.style.color = rem < 20 ? 'red' : 'black';
  });
</script>
```
A poor answer (using `keyup` instead of `input`, missing `maxlength` attribute, or not handling paste/drag-drop) shows the student doesn't know `input` event fires on all value changes (typing, paste, undo, drag-drop) while `keyup` misses non-keyboard input.
